  "primary_subject_agency_ids": [],
  "summary": "Flock Safety CEO Garrett Langley spoke at TechCrunch Disrupt in San Francisco, blaming misinformation for the backlash against the company's ALPR cameras while protesters rallied outside. He acknowledged mistakes including an audio-detection pilot, noted Flock has advised customers to shorten data retention from 30 to 7 days, and said no contracts have been canceled by Flock. The article references prior incidents including SFPD's audit of 1.6 million out-of-state searches and a fired San Jose officer who misused the system.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "Much of the problem that I'm facing right now is this newer landscape where social media is so interwoven with politics and is so rife with misinformation",
      "paragraph_idx": 10
    },
    {
      "quote": "Langley said Flock reevaluated its processes in August and made changes, including advising customers to reduce their data storage time from 30 days to seven.",
      "paragraph_idx": 25
    },
    {
      "quote": "A September 2025 Standard investigation found that San Francisco's Flock license-plate database had been accessed 1.6 million times by out-of-state agencies, likely illegally.",
      "paragraph_idx": 32
    }
  ],
